# Factoring and design

## Small words

Each colon definition does **one** thing at the level of its name and fits on
one or two lines (about seven references). If you need an inline comment to
track what is on the stack mid-definition, factor.

```forth
\ BAD -- magic 255, no named steps
: send-packet ( c-addr u -- )  dup 255 min over c! 1+ swap 1- ... ;

\ GOOD
255 constant max-payload
: clamp-payload ( u -- u' )                max-payload min ;
: store-byte    ( c-addr c -- c-addr' )    over c! 1+ ;
: advance       ( c-addr u -- c-addr' u' ) 1 /string ;
```

## Lexicons and components

Group related words into a **component** that exposes a small **lexicon**;
everything else stays internal. A bare literal inside the component is fine;
once it crosses the boundary, name it (`14 constant /record`). One name per
function -- the Forth dictionary is itself a case statement.

## Factoring techniques

- **Data.** Pass the varying value: `: two-thirds ( n -- n' )  2 3 */ ;`.
- **Function.** Do not pass `( flag -- )` to pick between two bodies -- see
  `forth-anti-patterns.mdc`.
- **Code inside a conditional.** Lift the common tail out (`… 0= if drop
  [char] . then emit`, not `emit` on both arms).
- **Names.** Replace a run like `0sts 1sts … 7sts` with one defining word
  plus an index (`8 array status   ( i -- 'cell )`).
- **The control structure.** When two words differ only in the action body,
  factor the condition: `: ready? ( -- flag )  saturday? work-done? and ;`.
- **Bodies.** Identical bodies varying only in compile-time data become one
  `create … does>` family (see `forth-defining.mdc`).

## When to factor

- Definition exceeds about one line / seven references.
- You feel the urge to write a stack picture mid-definition -- cut at the
  picture and promote it to the new word's `( -- )`.
- Same three-word phrase appears twice (rule of three).
- You cannot give the fragment a single non-hyphenated name -- the concept
  is fuzzy; rethink rather than name.
- A `do … loop` becomes doubly nested -- move the inner loop into its own
  word.

Do not factor for its own sake: clichés like `over + swap` (turn `c-addr u`
into `do`'s `( limit start )`) read better inline than behind a name.

## `variable` as a handle between words

When several helpers must share one node index or pointer and the **public** word has a fixed stack effect, a `variable` beats `pick` / deep `>r`:

```forth
variable cur-node

: touch-node ( node -- )
  dup cur-node !
  ... unlink/link using cur-node @ ... ;

: get-key ( key -- val true | false )
  find-key if
    dup cur-node !
    cur-node @ item@
    cur-node @ touch-node
    drop true
  else
    false
  then ;
```

Name it for the role (`cur-node`, `ed-pair`), not `tmp`. Drop leftovers before the final flag/result so `T{ }T` depth matches.

## Calculation > data structure > logic

Pick the simplest adequate form: closed-form calculation, then a table
indexed by input, then `if` / `case`. `: bump ( n -- n' )  1- 2* 10 + ;`
beats a 3-arm `case` for "map 1 2 3 -> 10 12 14".

## Compile-time factoring

Let the compiler do the arithmetic; do not retype derived numbers. Size a
`,`-built table from `here`. Case statements and decision tables fall under
the same idea.

```forth
8 constant wide   4 constant ave
wide 3 *  ave 2 * +  80 swap -  2/  constant leftmargin

create points  10 ,  11 ,  12 ,  13 ,
here points -  cell /  constant #points
```

## Factor calculation out of display, and out of variables

A word that prints should not also compute. A word that hard-codes a
variable cannot be reused with a different start.

```forth
: (n.)  ( n -- c-addr u )  s>d swap over dabs <# #s rot sign #> ;
: n.    ( n -- )           (n.) type space ;
```
